 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (coupè with the print's grave accent, Tò!, davver, Lavoriam, the print's quotation marks around Rodolfo's report of Musetta's answer, «Non batte o non lo sento grazie al velluto che il copre», the brackets of the asides (Loiola va! Ti rodi e ridi.) and (Bugiardo, si strugge d'amor.), and the runs of dots after cuor, vidi, guarda). Loiola (Ignatius of Loyola) is Rodolfo's name for Marcello as a sly Jesuit; the print sets it without a comma before va!. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced. Translation literal, for meaning.",
